Quick Sort is a best case O(logn)
Quick Sort is a Recursive Sort

Quick Sort uses a Divide and Conq technique, much like a Binary Search

Binary Search, as a Reminder, partitions the data into a left and right
The Binary Search partitions until the partition contains only one element, or is null

Structural Partition: will partition the left side first, going all the way to the 
basecase.Once the Left side Partition is popped off, the sort will then
do the same for the Right side Partition.

Rec(LSide);
Rec(RSide);

This way of partitioning is known as a Structural Partition
Merge Sort utilizes a Structural Partition

Quick Sort Partitions using values that are less then or greater then the pivot value
The Pivot Value is not the same as the midpoint value
Quick SOrt uses Value/Static Partitioning

Quick Sort sorts in place, meaning memory allocation is O(1)
Merge Sort uses more memory then Quick Sort, O(n)

Merge Sort Worst Case: O(nlogn)
Quick Sort worst Case: O(n^2)

